title The lock that outlived its owner
prompt The Pheromone seat prompt under the baton -- roster cold then hot, reds first, rota row N mod 5, grade what you touch, read scope.
rota lap 3985, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears. All five rows had been read this day, so the modulus stood rather than advancing by hand. Read the threshold and its triad: the compass, the Lindy effect, the grain and the crossing.
think Aether's instruction is to listen for what the work is saying underneath its words, and the grain page states the ear's hard rule out loud: a guard that cannot red guards nothing, a witness must be proven able to make a sound before its silence means anything. I turned that on my own lane and found 70 Glow-family witnesses no runner reaches, of which 35 are the lowering family -- glow_lower and glow_rune, the runes and their lowering, which is this seat's whole subject. That was the lap I meant to take.
think The lap the tree actually had was underneath it. The cold roster stopped at 96 lines for half an hour, and ps showed glow_run_worker.sh on gate-lantern-face-text-u32 at 0.0% CPU with a sleep 1 child -- waiting, not computing. stat read the build lock's mtime at 17:16:57 against a clock reading 23:02, and its pid file at zero bytes. Five hours and forty-five minutes.
think The root is one character of test. lock_acquire writes the owner's pid INSIDE the lock directory, because mkdir is the atomic step and nothing can be written into a directory that does not exist yet. So a window exists -- microseconds -- where the lock stands and pid is empty, and the function waits on that window rather than reaping it, since reaping a lock being born hands two holders one lock. That reading is exactly right for a lock being born and exactly wrong for a lock whose owner died inside the window. The gate is [ -s "$lock/pid" ], false for a zero-byte file, so the reaping branch below it was never entered at all.
think The repair had to keep the seated property rather than trade it away. Presence cannot tell the two states apart, and time can: an empty or non-numeric pid earns a bounded grace of consecutive one-second observations, default five, and is reaped past it. The counter RESETS the moment a pid appears, so a lock released and retaken under a waiter's nose is never reaped for its predecessor's silence.
think I wrote the trade into the code rather than leaving it to be discovered. The grace opens a microsecond-wide window where a live owner could be reaped, against a CERTAIN permanent hang; every caller already emits to a private path and installs with an atomic rename, so a rare double-build leaves a whole binary either way. Safety before speed is TAME's order and a guaranteed deadlock is the larger safety failure.
obs THE ROSTER PRICED IT ITSELF. lantern_face green 1454s against the 9.5s its own roster row declares -- 67% of a 2,173-second pass on one dead lock, with nine more Glow guards queued behind it owing 1,800s each. The row's 9.5s was never wrong. A guard that declares 9.5s and reads 1454s reads as a slow guard, and it was a fast guard waiting on a dead lock: the cost landed on the guard's name rather than the lock's, so the number that was wrong pointed at the one thing that was right.
obs THE CONTROL ALREADY ASSERTED THE DEFECT AS INTENT. Two seated legs prove an empty and a non-numeric pid are waited on rather than reaped, and both are correct -- inside the grace. Both bounds are 1, shorter than the default grace of 5, so both keep their bytes and both keep their meanings; the five new legs prove the other half of the same reading. This is %430's shape from last lap in a second material: the repair reaches the input, and the seated case is freed exactly as before.
obs THE TWO TIMING LEGS VOTED BEFORE I MADE THEM MEASURE. Run against the elder function the first draft missed three of five -- and the two that passed did so vacuously, because the elder acquire simply failed after its whole 30-second bound, making elapsed >= 2 and elapsed >= 4 trivially true. A leg that passes under both the defect and the repair cannot tell them apart. An upper bound on each turned both into bites: elder pass=32 fail=5 verdict=behavior_missed, repair pass=37 fail=0. That the elder still reads exactly 32 is the other half of the proof -- I added nothing to its reading and removed nothing.
obs THE NUMBER MOVED FOUR TIMES BETWEEN THE FETCH AND THE SEND. I read --next as 439 at 23:07 and booked it; three peers then landed nine rows in ninety minutes, taking 439, 440, 441, 442, 443 and 444 out from under me across three rebases, and the row is %445. A published number never moves and mine was unshared, so it moved instead, six citations with it each time -- and the peer's own %442 citation in the same file had to be left alone, which a blanket sed would have corrupted. Fetch-before-book is not one act at the open; it is a reading that goes stale at the speed of the fleet, and the allocator refusing to answer mid-rebase is the guard that says so.
obs %440 FIRED AGAIN ON THE MERGE THAT LANDED IT. A peer booked %440 this hour for a rebase leaving the day shelf out of its own newest-first order, and rebasing onto that very commit did it twice more -- their 231916 and 230135 rows came through below 224930, and mine below both. Three rows out of place in one merge, and the guard beside them reads row BYTES rather than row ORDER, so it stayed GREEN through all of it. Sorted by hand and left as a finding: an index-order reading is the guard that fault still wants.
obs WHAT I DID NOT DO. The 35 unheard lowering witnesses stay unheard tonight; a second Glow choir is the lap they want and it is a lap, not a footnote. The roster's two reds are both already booked and both gate-held -- agent_jail (%422) and commit_message_guard (%431, SEED_PUBLISHER_ARMS 0) -- so neither is new and neither is mine to close.
obs THE REPAIR IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. Same roster, same tree, one lap apart: lantern_face 1454s -> 42s and the whole pass 2,173s -> 800s. The 1,412 seconds the guard stopped paying and the 1,373 the pass stopped paying are the same seconds read from two places, which is what makes the reading a measurement rather than a coincidence. The 42s still stands above the row's declared 9.5s, and I am not claiming that row exact: the 9.5 was measured on the field Mac 20260830.011217, this is the Dallas pier, and a fourfold difference between two machines is ordinary. Naming it beats quietly rounding it into agreement.
